1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,350
The Curiosity Club.

2
00:00:02,350 --> 00:00:04,769
Episode 35.

3
00:00:04,769 --> 00:00:06,956
The Triangle Team.

4
00:00:06,956 --> 00:00:09,673
Here is something to listen for.

5
00:00:09,673 --> 00:00:13,614
What did Maya add to help the square frame keep its shape?

6
00:00:13,614 --> 00:00:15,040
Ready?

7
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:17,119
Let us find out.

8
00:00:17,119 --> 00:00:21,684
The club builds shapes with strips of cardboard joined at the corners.

9
00:00:21,684 --> 00:00:23,967
Leo makes a square.

10
00:00:23,967 --> 00:00:26,385
Maya makes a triangle.

11
00:00:26,385 --> 00:00:30,570
Pip declares the square the winner because it has more corners.

12
00:00:30,570 --> 00:00:34,374
Ms. Park asks them to push gently on the shapes.

13
00:00:34,374 --> 00:00:39,035
The square changes into a slanted shape as its corner joints turn.

14
00:00:39,035 --> 00:00:44,021
Maya's triangle keeps its shape more easily without bending the strips.

15
00:00:44,021 --> 00:00:48,519
The number of corners was not a useful test, Pip admits.

16
00:00:48,519 --> 00:00:52,282
Maya adds a diagonal strip across the square.

17
00:00:52,282 --> 00:00:58,179
Now it is divided into two triangles, and it resists the same gentle push better.

18
00:00:58,179 --> 00:01:02,255
They look at pictures of bridge frameworks and roof supports.

19
00:01:02,255 --> 00:01:05,176
Triangles appear again and again.

20
00:01:05,176 --> 00:01:08,614
Does that mean every bridge must be a triangle?

21
00:01:08,614 --> 00:01:10,557
Leo asks.

22
00:01:10,557 --> 00:01:13,192
No, says Ms. Park.

23
00:01:13,192 --> 00:01:16,616
Engineers use many kinds of structures.

24
00:01:16,616 --> 00:01:21,888
The material, connections, shape, and expected loads all matter.

25
00:01:21,888 --> 00:01:25,991
The club improves a cardboard tower for its exhibition.

26
00:01:25,991 --> 00:01:31,711
They add braces where the frame was leaning and test it with the same small load as before.

27
00:01:31,711 --> 00:01:35,108
Pip designs a banner: Team Triangle.

28
00:01:35,108 --> 00:01:37,730
Excellent at standing still.

29
00:01:37,730 --> 00:01:42,907
Leo asks whether he should make his sandwich triangular for extra strength.

30
00:01:42,907 --> 00:01:47,133
That depends on your engineering goal, Maya replies.

31
00:01:47,133 --> 00:01:49,374
My goal is to eat it.

32
00:01:49,374 --> 00:01:56,630
Then cutting it into triangles may help your lunch, she says, but it will not prove anything about bridge safety.

33
00:01:56,630 --> 00:02:02,880
Pip records another important design lesson: Always ask what problem you are trying to solve.

34
00:02:02,880 --> 00:02:05,788
Now, a little more to discover.

35
00:02:05,788 --> 00:02:14,388
A triangle made of fixed-length sides is geometrically rigid, while a four-sided frame with freely turning corners can change shape.

36
00:02:14,388 --> 00:02:18,790
A diagonal brace can divide such a frame into triangles.

37
00:02:18,790 --> 00:02:27,595
Real structural strength also depends on materials, connections, and loads; shape alone is not a complete safety test.

38
00:02:27,595 --> 00:02:30,434
Let us hear the main idea again.

39
00:02:30,434 --> 00:02:33,641
The square frame shifted at its joints.

40
00:02:33,641 --> 00:02:37,622
A diagonal brace made it resist that movement better.

41
00:02:37,622 --> 00:02:44,769
The children connected the model with real structures while remembering that engineering involves more than a single shape.

42
00:02:44,769 --> 00:02:47,744
Here are three useful expressions.

43
00:02:47,744 --> 00:02:50,869
You can listen, or say them with me.

44
00:02:50,869 --> 00:02:53,736
What problem are we trying to solve?

45
00:02:53,736 --> 00:02:56,603
What problem are we trying to solve?

46
00:02:56,603 --> 00:02:59,402
This brace helps support the frame.

47
00:02:59,402 --> 00:03:02,201
This brace helps support the frame.

48
00:03:02,201 --> 00:03:05,027
Let us test it with the same load.

49
00:03:05,027 --> 00:03:07,853
Let us test it with the same load.

50
00:03:07,853 --> 00:03:10,448
Remember our listening question?

51
00:03:10,448 --> 00:03:14,388
What did Maya add to help the square frame keep its shape?

52
00:03:14,388 --> 00:03:16,521
Here is the answer.

53
00:03:16,521 --> 00:03:21,752
She added a diagonal brace, dividing the square into two triangles.

54
00:03:21,752 --> 00:03:25,407
And here is a question just for your imagination.

55
00:03:25,407 --> 00:03:29,823
Where can you find triangles used as part of a structure around you?

56
00:03:29,823 --> 00:03:32,934
There is more than one possible answer.

57
00:03:32,934 --> 00:03:36,182
Keep wondering, and see you next time.
